      "writtenOn": "2024-10-23T22:30:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I respectfully disagree!\n\nThe DISCONNECTING state is an indicator of the work being done to go from being connected to being disconnected. This often means cleaning up after yourself. IMO, it does not need to be tied to the actual link state. There\u0027s a reason why we have that state in our BluetoothProfile APIs, and there\u0027s a reason why many profiles use and broadcast on the state. Its also not used to imply incoming or outgoing connection state transitions (we could introduce an EXTRA for this though). \n\nSometimes there\u0027s lots of clean up work to be done (waiting on a timeout, cleaning up data, broadcasting things) and other times there\u0027s no work to do (pass on through).\n\nWhat we broadcast doesn\u0027t strictly HAVE to match the implementation\u0027s state of things either. We _can_ present a well formed facade to consumers of the platform, even if internally a connection was brutally torn down!\n\nWhile many apps may not care about the DISCONNECTING state, I\u0027m sure some do. Its been an API since the beginning effectively.\n\nAs well, from an HFP point of view, Disconnecting as a state seems desirably. From a metrics point of view too, we were told passing through the state is desirable/preferred.",
      "parentUuid": "6fd976d2_fb2e5629",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1972,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 1973,
        "endChar": 44
      },
      "revId": "f352c31c5318c9a810f7095a88a04deaf24fe355",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
